{"subjectId":"java","id":"collections-utilities","title":"Collections & Arrays Utilities","group":"Collections Framework","order":49,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>java.util.Collections</code> and <code>java.util.Arrays</code> are static toolboxes — sorting, searching, filling, copying, wrapping. Knowing them saves writing (and debugging) loops.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i><code>Collections</code> algorithms</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; xs = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(5, 3, 9, 1, 3));\n        Collections.sort(xs);\n        System.out.println(xs + \" binarySearch(5)=\" + Collections.binarySearch(xs, 5)\n                + \" binarySearch(4)=\" + Collections.binarySearch(xs, 4));\n        Collections.reverse(xs);\n        System.out.println(xs + \" max=\" + Collections.max(xs) + \" min=\" + Collections.min(xs));\n        System.out.println(\"frequency(3)=\" + Collections.frequency(xs, 3));\n        Collections.swap(xs, 0, 4);\n        System.out.println(xs);\n        Collections.rotate(xs, 1);\n        System.out.println(xs);\n        Collections.shuffle(xs, new Random(42));   // seeded -&gt; reproducible\n        System.out.println(xs);\n        System.out.println(Collections.nCopies(3, \"ab\") + \" \" + String.join(\"\", Collections.nCopies(3, \"-\")));\n        System.out.println(Collections.disjoint(List.of(1, 2), List.of(3, 4)));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[1, 3, 3, 5, 9] binarySearch(5)=3 binarySearch(4)=-4\n[9, 5, 3, 3, 1] max=9 min=1\nfrequency(3)=2\n[1, 5, 3, 3, 9]\n[9, 1, 5, 3, 3]\n[1, 5, 3, 3, 9]\n[ab, ab, ab] ---\ntrue</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>binarySearch</code> returns <code>-(insertionPoint) - 1</code> when the key is missing: <code>4</code> would be inserted at index 3, so the result is <code>-4</code>. The list must already be sorted in the same order.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i>Wrappers and empty collections</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Collections.unmodifiableList(l)</code> (…<code>Set</code>, <code>Map</code>)</td><td>Read-only <strong>view</strong> — still reflects changes to <code>l</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Collections.synchronizedList(l)</code> (…<code>Map</code>)</td><td>Every method locked on one mutex; iterate inside <code>synchronized (list) { … }</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Collections.emptyList()</code> / <code>emptyMap()</code></td><td>Shared immutable empty instance</td></tr><tr><td><code>Collections.singletonList(x)</code></td><td>Immutable one-element list</td></tr><tr><td><code>Collections.addAll(c, a, b, c)</code></td><td>Add varargs to any collection</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;String&gt; src = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(\"a\"));\n        List&lt;String&gt; view = Collections.unmodifiableList(src);\n        List&lt;String&gt; copy = List.copyOf(src);\n        src.add(\"b\");\n        System.out.println(view + \" \" + copy);   // the view sees the change, the copy doesn't\n        try {\n            view.add(\"c\");\n        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"view is read-only\");\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[a, b] [a]\nview is read-only</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i><code>Arrays</code> helpers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] a = {5, 2, 8, 1};\n        Arrays.sort(a);\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a) + \" idx(5)=\" + Arrays.binarySearch(a, 5));\n\n        int[] filled = new int[4];\n        Arrays.fill(filled, -1);\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(filled));\n\n        int[] bigger = Arrays.copyOf(a, 6);            // pads with 0\n        int[] slice = Arrays.copyOfRange(a, 1, 3);     // [1, 3)\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(bigger) + \" \" + Arrays.toString(slice));\n\n        System.out.println(Arrays.stream(a).sum() + \" \" + Arrays.stream(a).max().getAsInt());\n        System.out.println(Arrays.equals(a, new int[]{1, 2, 5, 8}) + \" \" + (a == a.clone()));\n\n        int[][] grid = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};\n        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(grid));\n\n        Integer[] boxed = {3, 1, 2};\n        Arrays.sort(boxed, Collections.reverseOrder()); // comparators need object arrays\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(boxed));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[1, 2, 5, 8] idx(5)=2\n[-1, -1, -1, -1]\n[1, 2, 5, 8, 0, 0] [2, 5]\n16 8\ntrue false\n[[1, 2], [3, 4]]\n[3, 2, 1]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>Arrays.sort(int[])</code> uses dual-pivot quicksort (not stable, but stability is meaningless for primitives); <code>Arrays.sort(Object[])</code> uses stable TimSort. Primitive arrays can't take a <code>Comparator</code> — box them or sort then reverse.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Trap: <code>Arrays.asList</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String[] arr = {\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"};\n        List&lt;String&gt; fixed = Arrays.asList(arr);   // fixed-size view over the array\n        fixed.set(0, \"X\");                          // allowed, writes through\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));\n        try {\n            fixed.add(\"w\");\n        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"add -&gt; UnsupportedOperationException\");\n        }\n\n        int[] prim = {1, 2, 3};\n        System.out.println(Arrays.asList(prim).size());      // List&lt;int[]&gt; with ONE element\n        System.out.println(Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3).size());   // varargs of Integer\n\n        List&lt;String&gt; growable = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(Arrays.asList(arr));\n        growable.add(\"w\");\n        System.out.println(growable);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[X, y, z]\nadd -&gt; UnsupportedOperationException\n1\n3\n[X, y, z, w]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>Arrays.asList</code> returns a fixed-size list backed by the array (no <code>add</code>/<code>remove</code>), and <code>Arrays.asList(intArray)</code> gives a <code>List&lt;int[]&gt;</code> of size 1. Wrap it in <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(…)</code> for a normal list; use <code>Arrays.stream(ints).boxed().toList()</code> for primitives.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does Collections.synchronizedList not protect you from?</summary><div><p>Compound actions and iteration. Each single call is locked, but <code>if (!list.contains(x)) list.add(x)</code> can interleave between threads, and iterating requires manually holding <code>synchronized (list)</code>. Prefer <code>java.util.concurrent</code> collections.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>unmodifiableList vs List.copyOf vs List.of?</summary><div><p><code>unmodifiableList</code> is a read-only <em>view</em> — changes to the backing list show through. <code>List.copyOf</code> makes an independent immutable copy (returns the argument itself if it is already an immutable list). <code>List.of</code> builds an immutable list from values. Neither of the last two allows <code>null</code>s.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Which sort algorithms does Java use?</summary><div><p><code>Arrays.sort</code> on primitives: dual-pivot quicksort, O(n log n) average, not stable. On objects and in <code>Collections.sort</code>/<code>List.sort</code>: TimSort — a stable merge/insertion hybrid, O(n log n) worst case and O(n) on already-sorted data.</p></div></details></section>"}